WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:03.740
Sansa Stark
And Cersei Lannister:

00:00:03.740 --> 00:00:07.350
Two of Game of Thrones quintessential enemies.

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:12.530
Despite being on opposing sides of Westeros,
they have one thing in common: their clothing.

00:00:12.530 --> 00:00:16.369
In the final season of the show, days before
the Battle of Winterfell, both wear high braced

00:00:16.369 --> 00:00:18.380
necklines,
draped chains,

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:23.590
— sculptural shoulders that evoke armor,
even stud work along a central ridge detail.

00:00:23.590 --> 00:00:27.320
Their costumes are as connected as their plotlines
for most of the show.

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:31.279
And while some of the details can get lost
in the show’s dark motif,

00:00:31.279 --> 00:00:35.430
If you look closely, you’ll see how their
much their parallel outfits mirror their struggles

00:00:35.430 --> 00:00:37.050
for power and identity.

00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:39.090
CERSEI: And your dress.

00:00:39.090 --> 00:00:41.129
Did you make it?

00:00:41.129 --> 00:00:43.680
[SANSA nods]
CERSEI: Such talent.

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:52.899
You must make something for me.

00:00:52.899 --> 00:00:57.410
Take a look at Cersei’s outfit here, at
King’s Landing in Season 1.

00:00:57.410 --> 00:01:00.579
Michelle Clapton — the show’s Emmy award-winning
costume designer —

00:01:00.579 --> 00:01:04.860
dressed Cersei in a lot of lightweight, pastel
dresses in the first two seasons - pinks,

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:06.470
pale purple, turquoise.

00:01:06.470 --> 00:01:11.450
Within the story, these dresses suggested
a feminine queen in understated luxury.

00:01:11.450 --> 00:01:15.510
But to the audience, they often signaled moments
she seemed powerless.

00:01:15.510 --> 00:01:19.670
TYRION: Now the entire North has risen up
against us.

00:01:19.670 --> 00:01:20.670
CERSEI: I tried to stop it.

00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:21.670
TYRION: Did you?

00:01:21.670 --> 00:01:22.670
You failed.

00:01:22.670 --> 00:01:27.100
Cersei’s pastels play against her Lannister
red dresses to contrast her moments of power

00:01:27.860 --> 00:01:30.480
CERSEI: Seize him.

00:01:32.180 --> 00:01:33.120
Cut his throat.

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:35.020
Wait.

00:01:36.020 --> 00:01:38.260
I've changed my mind, let him go.

00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:41.310
— with the colors she wore in her moments
of helplessness.

00:01:41.310 --> 00:01:44.700
Like this turquoise dress she wears while
watching her daughter Myrcella being sent

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:47.130
away to Dorne.

00:01:47.130 --> 00:01:50.780
Around the same time, Sansa was a hostage
of the crown, at the mercy of the Lannisters

00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:53.420
— wearing dresses that mirrored her captor
Cersei.

00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:58.100
The oversized, pastel hand-me-downs do the
same thing for her that they did for Cersei

00:01:58.100 --> 00:01:59.770
— reflect her lack of power.

00:01:59.770 --> 00:02:05.610
“Your grace, whatever my traitor brother
has done I had no part, you know that.

00:02:05.610 --> 00:02:07.040
I beg you, please!”

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:10.970
They’re both at sea, drowning
in light-colored fabric, and unable to change

00:02:10.970 --> 00:02:12.800
their circumstances.

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:17.720
The Battle of Blackwater changed all of that.

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:21.260
Before the battle, Cersei was dressed in the
most Lannister outfit she could find:

00:02:21.260 --> 00:02:26.420
a red dress with Lannister lions, and an ornamental
breastplate with, you guessed it,

00:02:26.420 --> 00:02:27.580
Lannister lions.

00:02:27.590 --> 00:02:30.010
It didn’t end up being very effective for
morale,

00:02:30.010 --> 00:02:32.330
“The battle is lost your grace.”

00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:36.890
But it was a show of house power, and Sansa’s
costuming followed suit.

00:02:36.890 --> 00:02:41.420
In the wake of the battle, Sansa donned her
own version of a house color: purple.

00:02:41.420 --> 00:02:46.180
It wasn’t Stark blue, but it gave her more
identity than her pastel hostage hand-me-downs

00:02:46.180 --> 00:02:50.260
— without rocking the boat too much around
the red Lannisters.

00:02:50.260 --> 00:02:54.830
It’s an early example of a character dressing
in “survival camouflage,” to blend in

00:02:54.830 --> 00:02:56.440
with those who pose a threat.

00:02:56.440 --> 00:03:00.780
But Sansa’s costuming also shows how she’s
begun to assert herself — this Stark scarf

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:03.180
under her gowns is a subtle nod to home.

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:07.760
Cersei’s season 3 clothes doubled down on
Lannisterism, as she assumed the role of Queen

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:10.830
Mother: red everywhere, increasingly ornate
fabrics,

00:03:10.830 --> 00:03:13.040
and lion sigil armor and jewelry.

00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:16.340
“You’re a clever man, but not half as
clever as you think you are.”

00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:20.870
And later in the season, Sansa’s costuming
echoed this shift at her wedding to Tyrion

00:03:20.870 --> 00:03:21.870
Lannister.

00:03:21.870 --> 00:03:25.480
“You may now cloak the bride and bring her
under your protection.”

00:03:25.480 --> 00:03:30.820
Her gown was Sansa purple, but with armored
panniers — a distinctly Cersei touch.

00:03:30.820 --> 00:03:34.620
And the show’s embroiderer, Michelle Carragher,
included some tricky embellishments,

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:37.110
Featuring lions overcoming Stark wolves.

00:03:37.110 --> 00:03:41.600
The embroidered scene mimicked the way Cersei’
Lannister lion signaled authority —

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:44.880
— but made sure there was something Stark
on Sansa’s dress, too.

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:49.540
Their dueling sigils show how much Sansa had
already learned from Cersei about using clothing

00:03:49.540 --> 00:03:50.910
to signal alliances.

00:03:50.910 --> 00:03:54.569
An especially important skill moving forward.

00:03:54.569 --> 00:03:59.490
In season 4, as the two characters diverge
— with Cersei vying for power behind the

00:03:59.490 --> 00:04:03.849
throne, and Sansa grappling with her identity
— those signature colors begin to disappear.

00:04:03.849 --> 00:04:07.560
Cersei goes dark as she mourns her son, Joffrey.

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:08.569
How dark?

00:04:08.569 --> 00:04:14.300
Well, that house sigil is either studded with
skulls or half-rotted, so... Pretty damn dark.

00:04:14.300 --> 00:04:18.650
Sansa’s transformation happens after Petyr
Baelish rescues her from King’s Landing

00:04:18.650 --> 00:04:23.200
— and her new circumstances prompt a dress
so important we see her making it.

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:25.190
Technically, the dress signaled Sansa going

00:04:25.190 --> 00:04:27.120
undercover as Petyr’s “daughter.”

00:04:28.340 --> 00:04:29.960
“I know what you want."

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:31.180
"Do you?”

00:04:34.540 --> 00:04:39.000
But the details — straight sleeves like
Baelish’s coats, feathers evoking his nickname,

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:43.060
“the Mockingbird,” and a gunmetal palette
— were pivotal, reflecting Sansa’s keen

00:04:43.060 --> 00:04:45.160
awareness of survival camouflage.

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:46.940
A skill she learned under Cersei.

00:04:46.940 --> 00:04:50.460
Even their necklaces are echoes, a little piece of armor at the throat.

00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:54.480
As Cersei and Sansa begin to reassert their power around season 6,

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:56.980
their gowns become increasingly stiff and severe

00:04:56.980 --> 00:05:00.580
and house loyalty returns to their costuming with a vengeance.

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:06.820
During Sansa’s Northern tour in season 6 to rally support against Ramsay Bolton,

00:05:06.820 --> 00:05:12.180
she wears the Westerosi version of a graphic tee, Stark direwolf front and center.

00:05:12.180 --> 00:05:14.560
JON: New dress?

00:05:14.560 --> 00:05:16.640
SANSA: I made it myself, do you like it?

00:05:18.440 --> 00:05:19.800
JON: I like the wolf bit.

00:05:20.660 --> 00:05:24.320
The outfit is a clear indicator of how far she’s come into her own power —

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:27.940
evidenced as she watches Ramsay being eaten by his own dogs.

00:05:30.020 --> 00:05:34.620
The same way Cersei’s sigil signified authority after her violent bid for the throne.

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:42.340
With no further use for the survival camouflage of feminine queen or splendid Queen Mother,

00:05:42.340 --> 00:05:46.680
Cersei’s gowns echo Lannister armor, with high-necked silhouettes in forbidding fabrics,

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:50.920
and defensive details like epaulets — reminders of the family’s military might.

00:05:50.920 --> 00:05:54.840
And by the time Sansa's back in Winterfell on her own terms in season 7,

00:05:54.840 --> 00:05:58.200
Her wardrobe is high-necked silhouettes in forbidding fabrics.

00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:03.260
The epaulets and signature necklace echo Stark armor, while feathers represent her time with Baelish,

00:06:03.260 --> 00:06:07.460
reflecting the Stark family’s prestige and the cunning she uses to govern the North.

00:06:07.460 --> 00:06:11.580
Since the women parted at the Purple Wedding, they’ve kept pace with each other — strategically —

00:06:11.580 --> 00:06:16.640
SANSA: Cersei told you her army was coming north to fight for you?

00:06:16.640 --> 00:06:18.420
TYRION: She did.

00:06:18.420 --> 00:06:21.120
SANSA: And you believed her?

00:06:22.280 --> 00:06:23.560
— and visually.

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:29.940
And by the show’s final season, Sansa and Cersei have amassed different kinds of authority

00:06:29.940 --> 00:06:33.440
and have taken different lessons to heart about what wins a war.

00:06:34.860 --> 00:06:38.920
SANSA: While I ensured our stores would last through winter, I didn't account for Dothraki,

00:06:38.920 --> 00:06:42.400
Unsullied and two full-grown dragons.

00:06:43.380 --> 00:06:45.000
CERSEI: Twenty-thousand men, is it?

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:46.260
HARRY: Yes, Your Grace.

00:06:46.260 --> 00:06:47.960
CERSEI: And elephants?

00:06:47.960 --> 00:06:50.740
HARRY: Uh, no elephants, Your Grace.

00:06:51.200 --> 00:06:52.780
CERSEI: That’s disappointing.

00:06:52.780 --> 00:06:56.460
Despite the diverging plotlines, as both of them enter the final battle,

00:06:56.460 --> 00:07:00.500
their costumes show their similarities rather than emphasize their differences.

00:07:00.500 --> 00:07:05.660
In the end, they stand on opposite sides, with their costumes clearly echoing each other.

00:07:05.660 --> 00:07:11.520
And as the show draws to a close — it’s safe to say those parallels are there for a reason.

